  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Smt. Bhavna Gupta vs The State on 22 March, 2023 In The Court Of Mr. Dharmesh Sharma Principal District & Sessions Judge / Food Safety Appellate Tribunal; New Delhi District Patiala House Courts : New Delhi Fsat No. 11/2022 Cnr No. Dlnd010063372022 Smt. Bhavna Gupta W/o Sh. Anil Kumar Gupta Proprietor of Bankey Bihari Dairy 23/9, Shakti Nagar Opposite Aggarwal Hospital Delhi110007. And Resident of : 18/17, Shakti Nagar, Delhi110007. .....Appellant Versus The State Govt. of Nct of Delhi Through Food Safety Officer, Department of Food Safety 8th Floor, Mayur Bhawan Behind Super Bazar, Connaught Place New Delhi110001. ...... Respondent",
    "facts": "namely Smt. Bhavana Gupta, W/o Sh. Anil Kumar Gupta and he identified himself and discloses his intention for taking sample of \"Cow Desi Ghee\" for analysis . It is stated that no public witness came forward but Sh. Anil Kumar, Process Server was joined as a witness to the proceedings and at about 3:35pm, samples of \"Cow Desi Ghee\", which was meant for human consumption and being sold from the aforesaid address were taken; and that the sample consisted of four plastic jars, each containing 500 grams of \"Cow Desi Ghee\" (ready for sale for human consumption) as originally sealed and capped plastic jars by the FBO bearing no label declaration but disclosed to be of the same batch to the FSO and that each plastic jar containing the sample was marked, packed, fastened and sealed as per the prescribed procedure and the signatures of the FBO were obtained vide prescribed Form VA and Panchnama was done at the spot and a sum of Rs.1400/ was paid towards the cost of four plastic jars, each containing 500 grams of \"Cow Desi Ghee\". It was the case of the department that on 17.01.2020, one part of the aforesaid sample of \"Cow Desi Ghee\" bearing sample code number 128/1054/11/2020 with Designated Officer Code number 01/DO20/15667 in intact condition along with copy of memo in Form VI under a sealed packet along with another copy of memo in Form VI under sealed cover were sent separately to the Food Analyst, Delhi. 3. To cut the long story short, the Food Analyst vide its report bearing No. FSS/182/2020 dated 27.01.2020 reported and opined that the sample was 'misbranded' because there was violation of Regulation 2.2.1.7, 2.2.2.1, 2.2.2.4, 2.2.2.6, 2.2.2.7, 2.2.2.8, 2.2.2.9 & 2.2.2.10 of the Food Safety & Standards (Packaging & Labelling) Regulation, 2011 (in short \"FSS (P&L) Regulation). A copy of the Food Analyst Report was sent to the FBOcumProprietor Smt. Bhavana Gupta on 04.02.2020. As per the original record of the ld AO/ADM, the appellant was afforded an opportunity to file an appeal against the report of the Food Analyst under Section 46(4) of FSS Act for sending one part of the sample to the Referral Food Laboratory (in short \"RFL\"), if so desired, but the said option was not exercised. Accordingly, a complaint was filed for taking action against the appellant. Notice was issued by the Ld. AO to the appellant and it is borne out from the original record produced before this Court that appearance was put by Sh. Shailesh, AR of Smt. Bhavana Gupta on 09.08.2021 as well as 23.08.2021 in terms of the authority letter issued by the FBO, and on latter date, a reply was filed in which she pleaded guilty and requested for taking a lenient view. The AR of the appellant then appeared on 06.09.2021, on which date, the matter was reserved for orders and eventually, the impugned order was passed on 19.04.2022. 4. It is the case of the appellant that she received the copy of the impugned order on 06.05.2022 and then the present appeal was instituted on 26.07.2022. Surprisingly, although the appellant admits that she is the sole proprietor of M/s Bankey Bihari Dairy in question, she has given an altogether different version of the story alleging that some person visited her shop on 16.01.2020 and purchased open ghee from her without disclosing his identity and it was later on that the FSO identified himself and intimidated her and her staff and forced them to sign certain blank documents and she only received communication from the Ld. AO/ADM for appearance on Smt. Bhavna Gupta vs The State on 22 March, 2023 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/65674291/ 2 26.07.2021 and later appeared on 09.08.2021 and then on 23.08.2021 and she was shocked to receive the impugned order dated 19.04.2022 holding the appellant guilty and imposing penalty of Rs. One Lakh. GROUNDS OF APPEAL: 5. The impugned order is assailed inter alia on the ground that the Ld. AO/ADM she was denied and not afforded with an opportunity of fair hearing; and that Analyst Report dated 27.01.2020 was supplied to her vide letter bearing no. F.121(24)/DO Central/FS/2020/12340 dated 04.02.2020; and that no opportunity was given to her for sending the 4th packet for analysis to a NADL accredited laboratory in terms of Section 47(1)(c)(3) of FSS Act; and that in any case as per Regulation 2.4.5 of Act, there was a mandate upon the FSO to send the sample to a NADL accredited laboratory, which was not done; and that the FSO failed to specify as to how much quantity of the food item was sent to the Food Analyst and the opinion of Ld. AO of declaring the food item as misbranded under various FSS (P&L) Regulations was given in a mechanical and arbitrary manner. 6.",
